===Etymology===
{{unc|zlw-opl|title=Disputed}}. Probably ultimately {{der+|zlw-opl|gem|nocap=1}}. {{etydate|1476}}.
* Per Bruckner and Boryś, {{bor+|zlw-opl|de|brugge<t:bridge, street><q:Central dialects>|nocap=1|nocat=1}} with the semantic shift explained with the fact that bridge surfaces were generally hardened with stones.
* Per Mańczak, this is phonetically impossible, and claims {{bor+|zlw-opl|gmh|-|nocap=1|nocat=1}}, as the second element of {{cog|de-AT|Innsbruck}}.
* Per Bańowski, {{bor+|zlw-opl|gml|bruk<t:broken off piece of stone, etc.>|nocap=1|nocat=1}}, cognate with modern {{cog|de|Bruch}}.

